/Type /StructElem
/S /H6
/P 44 0 R
/T (Low-code platforms allow applications to be developed mainly through visual tools instead of large amounts of traditional program code. Applications are assembled from predefined components such as forms, reports and dashboards, and behaviour is defined through workflows, rules and configuration, with a small amount of scripting for specific needs. This reduces development time and effort and lets a wider range of people take part in building business applications.)
/E (Low-code platforms allow applications to be developed mainly through visual tools instead of large amounts of traditional program code. Applications are assembled from predefined components such as forms, reports and dashboards, and behaviour is defined through workflows, rules and configuration, with a small amount of scripting for specific needs. This reduces development time and effort and lets a wider range of people take part in building business applications.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
65 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H6
/P 44 0 R
/T (Low-code platforms suit standard business applications. As the requirements of an enterprise grow in users, data volume, process complexity, integrations, security and customisation, the built-in capabilities of a platform may no longer be sufficient on their own. This case study examines such limitations by scaling up the Event Registration application from a single technical symposium to an enterprise-wide event platform.)
/E (Low-code platforms suit standard business applications. As the requirements of an enterprise grow in users, data volume, process complexity, integrations, security and customisation, the built-in capabilities of a platform may no longer be sufficient on their own. This case study examines such limitations by scaling up the Event Registration application from a single technical symposium to an enterprise-wide event platform.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
