281 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 50 0 R
/T (As the organisation grows, requirements become more demanding. Large numbers of participants and events raise performance and data-management concerns; integration with payment, CRM and inventory systems raises integration concerns; differing access needs and the handling of personal data raise security and governance concerns; and advanced features such as attendance forecasting need external services. In such cases extensions, integrations or custom development are needed in addition to the low-code application. Low-code is therefore useful for rapidly developing standard business applications, whereas increasingly complex enterprise requirements call for a hybrid approach, and the choice should be based on the requirements of the application.)
/E (As the organisation grows, requirements become more demanding. Large numbers of participants and events raise performance and data-management concerns; integration with payment, CRM and inventory systems raises integration concerns; differing access needs and the handling of personal data raise security and governance concerns; and advanced features such as attendance forecasting need external services. In such cases extensions, integrations or custom development are needed in addition to the low-code application. Low-code is therefore useful for rapidly developing standard business applications, whereas increasingly complex enterprise requirements call for a hybrid approach, and the choice should be based on the requirements of the application.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
282 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 50 0 R
/T (Low-code platforms offer speed and simplicity for standard business applications, but their limitations become more significant as enterprise complexity increases. The trade-off is clear: low-code gives fast delivery, lower cost and wider participation in development, in exchange for ceilings in scale, logic, integration, customisation, governance and platform independence. For the Event Registration & Participant Management System, the standard functionality can be built quickly with low-code, while demanding requirements in logic, integration, security, scale and advanced technology need pro-code extensions connected to core enterprise systems. )
/E (Low-code platforms offer speed and simplicity for standard business applications, but their limitations become more significant as enterprise complexity increases. The trade-off is clear: low-code gives fast delivery, lower cost and wider participation in development, in exchange for ceilings in scale, logic, integration, customisation, governance and platform independence. For the Event Registration & Participant Management System, the standard functionality can be built quickly with low-code, while demanding requirements in logic, integration, security, scale and advanced technology need pro-code extensions connected to core enterprise systems. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
283 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 50 0 R
/T (A hybrid architecture keeps the speed of low-code for the standard layer and adds the flexibility of custom code where it is needed, which is why it is regarded as the practical standard for scalable enterprise platforms. It is therefore important to understand the limitations of a platform before selecting a development approach, so that the chosen approach matches the requirements of the enterprise application.)
/E (A hybrid architecture keeps the speed of low-code for the standard layer and adds the flexibility of custom code where it is needed, which is why it is regarded as the practical standard for scalable enterprise platforms. It is therefore important to understand the limitations of a platform before selecting a development approach, so that the chosen approach matches the requirements of the enterprise application.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
